JEREMY PRIES: We're
here today to talk

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.170
about Cloud costs and
reporting on them,

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.610
and hopefully aligning
that with what

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.680
services you're delivering
for your business

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.280
or your customers.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.520
The agenda today-- so I need
to go over some things with you

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.830
to make sure that everyone
understands the building

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.130
blocks that we have,
organizational options

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.720
about how we set up
our GCP infrastructure.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.450
Then we'll go over what's
available for tooling out

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.480
there.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.020
And then we can get
into some strategies

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.670
towards the end of
the presentation

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.120
about how we can apply those
and help you line up your costs.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.130
So I'm Jeremy, director of
Cloud infrastructure for Agosto.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:57.960
Agosto's been a GCP partner
for at least 10 years

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.800
since G Suite was
first released.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.838
Agosto started the Google
practice there and divested

00:01:05.838 --> 00:01:06.880
the rest of the business.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.660
And since then, we've
been strictly focused

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.730
on Google technologies.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.920
So my role in
infrastructure is to do

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.350
everything that's not
app dev or G Suite

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.700
or Chrome for the most part.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.070
So we help customers
all day, everyday

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.220
with aligning their costs
and identity management

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.262
and implementing these
various architectures.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.550
So I wanted to start out
with a couple of things

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.790
that I observe as I
work with our customers.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.292
First of all is that your
consumption-- everyone

00:01:41.292 --> 00:01:42.500
is in their own state, right?

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.005
But consumption starts at
zero, of course, and ramps up.

00:01:47.005 --> 00:01:48.380
So I'm trying to
get to the point

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.080
that it can surprise
you sometimes.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.170
Organizations want
predictable costs.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.060
They want to know, hey, what's
my bill going to be next month?

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.510
But Cloud isn't really
designed for that, ultimately.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.430
Because you pay
for what you use.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.560
It's not supposed to
be a predictable model.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.610
But our companies
still want predictable.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.650
So we try to meet in the middle
and estimate and use reporting

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.152
tools.

00:02:11.152 --> 00:02:13.610
And I put the quote down on
the bottom in number two there.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.770
Get this under
control is something

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.110
that we hear from our customers.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.530
Maybe you get started and
you fly under the radar

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.330
for your consumption.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.570
And then eventually,
you hit dollar amount

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:30.110
that raises some alarms
within your organization.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.930
And hopefully,
before we get there,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.680
we can have some
reporting that shows you,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.160
why did we get there?

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.510
Just because it doesn't mean
our costs are out of control,

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.600
is what the point
I'm trying to say is.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.970
Sometimes customers just
haven't spent any time

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.670
on reflecting back to their
business outside of the IT

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:52.640
organization, or whoever is
responsible for primary GCP

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.607
administration.

00:02:53.607 --> 00:02:55.940
Sometimes they just haven't
shared the right information

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.350
with the business.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.630
So that's a lot
of why we're here.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:00.230
Point three.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.480
Is there are a lot of different
people in your organization

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.890
who may be interested
in this data

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.140
and they need different stuff?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.650
App owners' business
units need different stuff

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.460
than the finance department.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.880
Then maybe if you have central
IT-- not everybody does--

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.470
but maybe if you have central
IT, they need different things.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.830
And executives want
their view as well.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.310
A high-level way
of looking at it.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.960
So we'll cover tool
sets that'll help us

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.660
for all of those
different people

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:31.340
and build out a strategy
that is proactive here.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:34.493
So let's go through
some definitions.

00:03:34.493 --> 00:03:36.160
Hopefully, this isn't
too basic for you.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.340
But I want to make sure
everybody understood.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.960
In GCP, we have orgs.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.850
So that's your Agosto.com,
for example, for me.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:47.440
We can have any number of them.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.610
It's a DNS domain.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.770
They're free.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.390
Sometimes we want more than one.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.910
Sometimes we want
some isolated for GCP.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.400
Sometimes we want it to
be our primary domain.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.950
But we have the
concept of an org node.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.100
Inside that used to be projects.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.900
And projects have resources.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:10.750
So resources being Compute
Engine, VMs, Kubernetes, Cloud

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:15.930
SQL, any of your
different technologies.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.660
And in between,
there are folders.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.769
So folders are not super old.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:23.410
They haven't existed forever.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:27.560
And we're constantly
finding new ways to do them.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.040
And folders are something that's
been really helpful for us

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.000
recently.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.710
So simply, projects can reside
in folders but don't have to.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.910
So I put a couple
of data points here.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.680
I think Google, when
they designed them,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.190
would primarily tell you
that folders are there

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:46.622
for IAM enforcement point.

00:04:46.622 --> 00:04:48.580
So you can set up some
permissions on a folder.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:49.955
And then every
project you create

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:51.653
in there will inherit
those permissions,

00:04:51.653 --> 00:04:54.070
a lot like a folder structure
you'd find on a file system.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.090
But we're finding
other ways to use them

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:05.300
in terms of reporting as well.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.420
Projects are completely
separate from each other

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.790
but report into the same folder.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.640
Resources, then, are part of
the project, like I mentioned.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.220
And then important point
is that you don't have

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.610
to have a project in a folder.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.667
So if you stand up an
empty org node in GCP--

00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.500
I'm sure plenty of you
have seen this-- hey,

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.417
I don't have a folder.

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:25.052
I don't have to
have them at all.

00:05:25.052 --> 00:05:27.260
So then outside of all of
that tree that I just drew,

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.490
there are billing accounts.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:29.810
A lot of you may know
your billing account

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.102
as being something in your org.

00:05:31.102 --> 00:05:33.120
But it doesn't have
to be in there.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.390
And you don't have to have
just one billing account.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.580
But a billing account
is simply a bill.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.560
It's going to come
as its own bill.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.160
And the bill is
linked to projects.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.230
And so you can default things.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.330
But generally speaking,
the bill has projects.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.520
The billing account has
projects associated with it.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.600
And you can disable billing on
the individual project level

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.800
as well.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.040
Important to note that
billing accounts also have

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.380
their own IAM permission sets.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.350
So you can blend in
some folder permissions,

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.510
along with billing
account permissions,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.940
to come up with a
strategy to make

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.830
sure the right people
are assigning billing

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:14.740
to the right place.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:20.690
Switching gears a little bit.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:21.410
Our labels.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.710
When we come into cost
reporting, a lot of people

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.200
jump to the concept
of labels immediately.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.410
It's not our only strategy,
but it's a super important one.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.680
So you can label lots of things.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.060
For the purposes of
billing information,

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.370
you link them with a resource.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.380
So at the simplest
level, a GCE VM

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.150
has a key value
pair that you could

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:48.530
assign to it that is the label.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.680
So we can have a
bunch of them per.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.190
You can have a permissions set.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:57.170
So 64 labels per resource.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.330
Really billing
specific related here.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.370
I have a couple example
use cases in a minute.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.730
But labels based on
a team or cost center

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:08.210
are a super common way
to report on billing.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.630
That's one of the
default ways that Google

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.910
might look at them being used.

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:15.410
Here you go.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:17.660
A laundry list of things
that can be labeled.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.880
Probably not complete.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:25.340
But think of anything that
bills, that generates costs,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.005
has a SKU associated with
them, can be labeled.

00:07:29.005 --> 00:07:30.380
I think it's
probably important--

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.610
I don't think I cover
it in the next slide--

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.340
I think it's probably important
to tell you that projects

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.892
can be labeled as well.

00:07:35.892 --> 00:07:37.100
That's a newer functionality.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.440
But that label doesn't show
up in the billing data.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.080
So it's not like an
inheritance thing.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.350
I've seen people assume that,
hey, I labeled my project,

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:48.210
and I might use that for
various automation reasons.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:52.580
But I can't look at that
label for the project.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.180
When I look at billing for,
say, GCE, in the instance,

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.310
it wouldn't inherit the label.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.480
So you'll see stuff--

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:04.700
VM instances, images, persistent
disks, snapshots, BigQuery,

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.730
even individual
queries, Cloud SQL--

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:11.280
all the major products
have support for labels.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.630
Here's some example things that
you might apply to a resource.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:20.010
An owner, a team, an
environment, production,

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.340
dev test, whatever.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:27.220
An app name, server
type, OS image.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:30.400
And so their use case
isn't strictly for billing.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.620
You might apply some
kind of security policy

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:34.150
or some kind of
automation to that.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.039
You might use it for
scripting to find

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:40.000
all the VMs with a certain label
and do something with them.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.950
But for the purposes
of this conversation,

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.550
they're there to
help you report.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:47.433
GKE is super popular.

00:08:47.433 --> 00:08:48.850
This is a little
bit in the weeds.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:50.433
But I wanted to bring
it up, because I

00:08:50.433 --> 00:08:52.870
think it's important, coming
up here in a little bit,

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.600
to understand that
GKE has name spaces.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.370
And it can assign
labels to them.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:03.900
But in the billing data, I
can't see the labels outside

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.220
of the cluster.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:08.580
So you assign the labels
from a billing perspective

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:09.750
at the cluster level only.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.658
So you'll see, if you
have a bunch of stuff

00:09:11.658 --> 00:09:13.200
in a rather large
cluster, it'll look

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.540
like a jumbled-together mix.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.750
Multi-tenant people
in particular

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.910
will need to come up
with a strategy for how

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:21.750
to deal with this.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.000
So that sets the stage
of the different building

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.010
blocks we have within GCP.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:33.840
So now I wanted to go over,
what kind of data can we get at?

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.330
So at the billing account level,
we can export into BigQuery.

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.180
And I'd assume everyone's
familiar BigQuery.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:44.050
It's a platform-based
database that's ready to use.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:46.260
We can go into our
billing account settings

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.200
and set up to
export billing data

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.470
to whatever data set we choose.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:55.423
So if we were to have more
than one billing account,

00:09:55.423 --> 00:09:57.090
we would end up with
separate data sets.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:58.628
They wouldn't be mixed together.

00:09:58.628 --> 00:10:00.420
At a minimum, they'd
be in separate tables.

00:10:03.132 --> 00:10:05.590
So I wanted to give you a little
bit about what's in there.

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:09.980
Because it becomes really
important to understand that.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:12.890
BigQuery billing data is--

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:13.670
so let me back up.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.210
Your billing account, then,
at the end of every month,

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:18.030
you'll receive a CSV file.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.972
And a CSV file will have--

00:10:19.972 --> 00:10:22.430
for every project, you'll have
every single SKU that you're

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:25.610
using and the number of units
in the bill in the dollar

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:27.860
amount associated with that.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:32.060
So that's at this really
high-level glossy view.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.040
The BigQuery data is not that.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:36.860
The BigQuery data trickles
in throughout the month,

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:37.700
throughout the day.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:41.030
You'll have, I don't
know, thousands of rows

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:44.690
that show up in there throughout
the month as you're reporting.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:48.560
So I'll go over a few of
the different items that

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:49.350
are in there.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:54.240
So there'll be a usage start and
end time for every single row.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.970
It'll associate with
an invoice month.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.940
So the invoice months
and BigQuery do add up.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.310
If you do sums on it, and
you look at the month,

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:06.580
it will add up to exactly down
to the penny, to your bill.

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:11.200
A project, of course, is
associated with it, a service.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.550
So Compute Engine is a service.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.620
Cloud storage is a service.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.330
Within a service are SKUs.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.900
So persistent disk, for
example, is a SKU that

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.870
falls within Compute Engine.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.510
So I included a
link to the SKUs.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:31.610
But it's a crazy number of SKUs.

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.660
Now we talked about folders.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:38.230
Folder data shows up in BigQuery
in the ancestry numbers.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:40.260
That's what the column's called.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:44.200
That will be in the
form of an organization.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.715
And then separated by slash
will be your full folder path.

00:11:47.715 --> 00:11:49.590
So you could have up to
five things in there,

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:53.110
with four folders inside an org.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.050
Important to note,
those are numbers.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.060
If you look inside
the GCP console

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.570
and you explore your
resources, you'll see numbers.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:02.700
It's an identifier number.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.408
And you'll need to
look that up if you're

00:12:04.408 --> 00:12:07.110
doing any reporting on it.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.510
So that data's in there.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:10.500
Labels.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:14.220
Key value pairs are nested
data that's in there.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.590
Location, as in zone,
region, country,

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.620
whatever the resource is,
whatever the appropriate thing

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:20.160
is there.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.630
Cloud storage might show up
as a country, versus a VM

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:26.700
would show up in a
region and a zone.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.870
And then usage costs and credit.

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:33.090
So it'll have a unit.

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:35.880
It'll have dollar
amount for the cost.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.650
And then credits are
important to note

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.560
as you're looking through
whatever billing reporting

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:44.160
data you're looking at is.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.200
The credits are-- sustained
use discount will show up

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.370
as a credit, for example.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.985
So a SKU has a list price.

00:12:52.985 --> 00:12:54.990
And it'll multiply
by-- it ends up

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.948
multiplying by the second
throughout the month.

00:12:56.948 --> 00:12:58.407
And then halfway
through the month,

00:12:58.407 --> 00:12:59.880
you start to receive a discount.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.340
That discount shows up
as the form of a credit.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:08.380
So you have to add them
together to get your total cost

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.040
for that particular row.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.080
So this conversation is
important to bring up.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.720
The main tool you have
to look at this data

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.910
is right in your console.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:22.410
Console billing reports.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:23.400
No setup required.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:25.860
You just log in and can
see your billing reports.

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:28.830
I'm sure all of you have.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:31.890
You can view this data by a
number of different things.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:36.390
So you can filter by date,
range, project, product SKU,

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:37.590
labels, all the above.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.838
One thing that's not
in there are folders.

00:13:39.838 --> 00:13:41.380
I don't know if
that's coming or not.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:43.740
But it's not in there today.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.080
So it's a really detailed
way to look at something.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:49.410
You could use this
reporting to, say,

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:53.760
how much paid OS
licenses do I have

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.760
for last month in this
project, for example?

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.060
That's in there by
looking at the SKU data.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.260
And you also can group
that data together.

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:05.030
So you could look at, hey, how
much did that project cost?

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.530
Pretty much, you can explore
your data in any number of ways

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:08.250
that you want to.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:12.270
So super powerful tool
and really good for GCP

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.460
administrators to use.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.760
Our experience has been
that, as we get away

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.430
from people who use
GCP on a daily basis,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.620
the less they really
would want to go in there

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:23.910
and navigate into that.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:25.860
And that's where we
use separate reporting

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:27.030
for those kind of personas.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.390
So your GCP admins are going
to be totally fine figuring out

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.262
whatever they need to.

00:14:32.262 --> 00:14:34.470
One of my primary ways to
look when questions come up

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:38.780
is to just log into the billing
reporting and go look at it.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:44.750
So that's the base
to build off of.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:49.460
From a product perspective, Data
Studio is included with GCP.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:53.370
Data Studio connects to
BigQuery and numerous other data

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:53.870
sources.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:57.470
But for the purpose of this,
it'll hook up to BigQuery.

00:14:57.470 --> 00:15:00.170
And you can use it as
a visualization tool.

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:01.970
And the only thing you
pay for is whatever

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.780
BigQuery costs that you have.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:05.930
And so BigQuery is
super cheap for when

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:08.880
you're just looking at small
data sets like billing data.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.270
You're probably looking at
usage in the pennies range.

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:13.670
So it flies under
the radar there.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.280
And it pretty much ends up being
a free reporting tool for you

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:17.090
to build off of.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.550
I think another important
thing to say about Data Studio

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.120
is you can feed it a table.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.935
And you can use the UI
to design your queries.

00:15:26.935 --> 00:15:29.060
And you don't end up knowing
you're making queries.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:32.180
You don't have to sit
down and write SQL here

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:34.430
to make this happen.

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.840
At the end, I have a
link to a GitHub repo

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:39.970
that'll help you
with some queries.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.100
And we use a view to
drive our Data Studio,

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:47.690
because we want to expose
folder data by name.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:50.810
So I include a little
helper table there.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:54.890
That helper table, then, has
your folder numbers and names.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.740
And it does that link together
and presents the right folder

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.110
name for you.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.620
And then it includes
some scripts

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:04.730
on how to gather that data G
Cloud and PowerShell scripts

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:07.850
that'll help you do that.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.650
When we're talking
about visualization,

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.440
there's no reason to say that it
has to be Data Studio, though.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:16.610
If your organization
has whatever other data

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:19.700
presentation visualization
tools, that's cool.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:21.840
Many of them hook
up to BigQuery.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:22.890
It's very popular.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.190
So most of the major
players already

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:26.900
have support for BigQuery.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:30.560
So you don't have to
necessarily use Data Studio.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.930
But this is the one
included in GCP.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:35.050
And it's the one we go with.

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:38.210
And it also has Google
Auth built right into it.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:40.160
So we can control
who can see it.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:41.660
And we can share
it, just like we're

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.610
used to with our businesses
driven by G Suite.

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:51.690
Brand-new-- I think it might
have been formally released

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.130
yesterday--

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:55.080
it was in beta form,
and we've been using it

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.870
for quite a while, is the
Sheets data connector.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:02.190
So it's Google Sheets data
connector for BigQuery.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.099
And we can write a
query, plop it in,

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:07.109
just like you see in
the screenshot there.

00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:12.119
And then we have our accounting
people run their own queries.

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:14.940
And so they can pull up a month
and summarize the data however

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.572
we decided in the query.

00:17:16.572 --> 00:17:18.030
And then they have
immediate access

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.200
to that at the end of the month.

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.819
For accounting people, they seem
to really love tabular data.

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:34.170
So everything we've said
here doesn't address the fact

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.540
that people want--

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.330
how much did that VM cost me?

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.490
It's something that
comes up all the time.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:45.960
So the main way I would
tell you to probably do that

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.295
would be to label your VM.

00:17:47.295 --> 00:17:49.837
I think it's way faster than
what I'm showing you here.

00:17:49.837 --> 00:17:51.420
But I wanted to tell
you that the spot

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:55.200
to see usage data
per VM is set up

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:58.410
inside each project
in Compute Engine.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:02.290
And it'll export to CSV only.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.450
So we could pull together
our own reporting

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:07.330
if we wanted to that'll go
look at the units of the data

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:08.150
that we see here.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:10.990
So it'll be a whole bunch of
rows inside the sheet, one

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:12.970
for each object.

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:15.730
And it'll show you, how
many seconds did I use?

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:17.740
How many byte seconds
of RAM did I use?

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:19.840
Or how many seconds
of CPU did I use?

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:23.080
And it can use that at
the end of the month

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.420
to calculate how
much that VM costs.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.808
Now there's no tool
sets beyond that

00:18:27.808 --> 00:18:30.100
I'm aware of to help you
actually figure that cost out.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:31.750
So it's a little
bit painful to do.

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:35.110
You'd need to look up the
SKU and do the math yourself.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:37.600
Not the end of the world,
but it's a bit of work.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:39.970
And that's why I'd recommend,
if you do need that data,

00:18:39.970 --> 00:18:43.900
to go into a labeling
strategy to try to achieve it.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:47.370
Or, most of our customers
who are talking about that,

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.050
I usually turn the conversation
around a little bit

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:53.080
and try to focus less
on individual instances

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:55.480
and focus more on services.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:19:01.780
So that the concept of the VM
being a permanent thing that

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:03.390
exists forever--

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:04.893
it's not fluid.

00:19:04.893 --> 00:19:06.310
And so it's much
more a cloud-like

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:12.410
to look at things as
a dynamic resource.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:13.280
The VMs come and go.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:15.140
And they're all part of
delivering whatever service.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.480
And that's more of the level
we want to look at things.

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:26.780
So important to note.

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:29.660
Sometimes people ask,
what network traffic

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:30.960
did I have per VM?

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.680
And that's a legit need that
you have, and it's in there.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:36.110
You can look at the
inter and intra zone.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:39.820
And you can also look at
inbound load balancers as well.

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:43.200
Super cool.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.830
I haven't had much chance
to play with this here.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.982
I wanted to bring it up.

00:19:47.982 --> 00:19:49.940
Probably a bit of a hike,
but there's a session

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:53.090
immediately after
this one in Moscone

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:56.390
on this specific feature
that was just released.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:00.180
So GKE can dump to
BigQuery usage data.

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:04.980
So in the same data set, we
could take a GKE cluster,

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:07.750
and then we could
look at namespaces.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.510
And we mentioned
namespaces earlier there.

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:14.220
We can look at namespaces or
labels inside the GKE cluster.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.090
And we can join that
with the billing data.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:17.340
So it's just performance data.

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.060
But we join that with
the billing data.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.880
And you can see here Google
provided an example Data Studio

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:22.380
report.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:23.838
And they do have
a bunch of example

00:20:23.838 --> 00:20:26.710
Data Studio stuff to
go look at out there,

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.580
if you do some searches.

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:30.645
And maybe on GitHub, I
can include some links.

00:20:30.645 --> 00:20:32.520
I haven't done that yet,
but I could probably

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:34.475
do that to direct you
right to the report

00:20:34.475 --> 00:20:37.110
so you don't have to
hunt around for them.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:39.890
But this seems like it's
going to be a really cool way

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.773
to build up some additional
reporting that we didn't have.

00:20:42.773 --> 00:20:44.190
This was first
announced, I think,

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:46.155
late January in a blog post.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:55.240
So moving on to some strategies.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.560
I feel like at this point,
we've laid the groundwork.

00:20:57.560 --> 00:20:58.977
You understand the
building blocks

00:20:58.977 --> 00:21:00.640
that we have on
how to organize GCP

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:02.930
and then where we
can get to our data.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:04.480
So what do we do with that now?

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:05.520
How do we set it up?

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.312
With customers, every
time they come to us,

00:21:07.312 --> 00:21:09.460
want to know how many
orgs should I have?

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:10.825
How many projects should I have?

00:21:10.825 --> 00:21:14.333
Should I do everything
in one big GKE cluster?

00:21:14.333 --> 00:21:15.500
All those sort of questions.

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:17.792
And I'm not going to tell
you that we have the answers,

00:21:17.792 --> 00:21:19.480
because it's super situational.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:21.092
Customers want best practices.

00:21:21.092 --> 00:21:23.050
And I think we're at the
point where we can say

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:24.410
we have some best practices.

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:26.950
But it's not going to be a
recipe for you that tells you

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:28.390
exactly what you need.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:31.840
It's more of some
general guidance.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.370
And everybody's just so unique.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.470
And the platform's
so vast that there's

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:39.130
no way to tell you exactly
how you should do that.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.600
So I want to come back to this.

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:42.650
So consumption builds over time.

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:45.520
So let's proactively build
some reports for our people

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:49.667
so that they don't become
surprised by the bill.

00:21:49.667 --> 00:21:51.250
So that we don't
have people who think

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.280
our costs aren't under control.

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:57.280
The only people I think of that
I describe as not under control

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.720
are people who have lost
resources out there.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:01.030
And that's a real thing.

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:02.920
VM sprawl is what we
used to call it back

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:07.720
in the day in the VMware
world, where we've stranded

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:09.670
maybe disk files, for example.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:10.863
Persistent disks.

00:22:10.863 --> 00:22:13.030
I ran into a customer the
other day, $12,000 a month

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:16.380
worth of SSDs, sitting
there detached.

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.200
That stuff happens.

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.367
Those are the people that
I think are under control.

00:22:19.367 --> 00:22:21.430
Besides that, it's
usually a visibility issue

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:22.967
about, we don't
really know what's

00:22:22.967 --> 00:22:25.300
going on out there, because
you haven't really showed me

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:25.820
much data.

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:27.400
And I get a bill at
the end of the month,

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:28.608
and it's x number of dollars.

00:22:28.608 --> 00:22:30.067
And it's not where
I want it to be.

00:22:30.067 --> 00:22:32.270
And I'm kind of frustrated
that I have to pay that.

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:35.410
So it's important that
our different personas

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.580
there on the right have
their view of the world

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.440
and can explore that data.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.190
And I think it helps
build a lot of trust.

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:50.230
So we've presented two main ways
to summarize that data, labels

00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:51.160
and folders.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:56.950
So I laid out a couple of
points related to each.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:00.650
The labels are super flexible.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:02.860
They're built into all the
existing reporting tools.

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:05.410
They're very natural to use.

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.490
The main concern
we have with people

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:10.090
who go in with an
only labeling strategy

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:14.590
is that you have to be detailed
enough to get the labels there.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:15.760
It's that easy.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:19.000
And if you're doing manual
processes, it gets missed.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.340
So from a process
perspective, we always say,

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:23.050
we need to automate that.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:24.550
We need to provision
with Terraform.

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:29.080
We need to use any kind
of automation tool,

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.270
or people forget things,
especially the little

00:23:31.270 --> 00:23:34.690
tiny details like that.

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:38.620
Folders are a way to
automatically summarize

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:39.910
the data.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.950
As long as projects get
put in the right place,

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:45.640
they're going to be
reported on correctly.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.860
But the concern sometimes,
where they don't work out,

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:52.220
is IAM rules are not in line.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:57.610
So there's several
reasons why you

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:00.400
might say that costs needs
to be spread out differently.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:04.060
Because I can't have that
set of IAM rules on a folder.

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.963
It doesn't add up in the end.

00:24:06.963 --> 00:24:08.380
So that's where
that doesn't work.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:11.260
And I'll also tell you,
folders aren't super flexible.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:12.890
They're are high-level roll up.

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:15.972
So I think the next
one goes into--

00:24:15.972 --> 00:24:16.930
I call this enterprise.

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:17.770
Call it corporate.

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:23.740
Call it non-SaaS offerings.

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.590
So I gave an example here.

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:28.390
You can see, IT has a folder.

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:29.410
Finance has a folder.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:30.820
HR has a folder.

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:33.100
Sales has a folder.

00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:35.140
There are apps.

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:37.930
Apps as in projects
in those folders.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.630
And it's a very quick
way to say, all right,

00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:42.640
so someone running
systems for finance

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:47.500
only has project creator access
at the finance folder level.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:50.000
And we have a lot of
customers who are doing this.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.810
And then we know exactly
that that finance department

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:56.353
is going to end up paying the
bill for that consumption.

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:05.117
It allows for three
layers of folders.

00:25:05.117 --> 00:25:06.075
I covered that earlier.

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:10.563
Cory, do you remember,
is it three or four?

00:25:10.563 --> 00:25:11.480
I thought it was four.

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.765
CORY: You could do three
or four in each of them.

00:25:13.765 --> 00:25:14.890
JEREMY PRIES: Yeah, thanks.

00:25:14.890 --> 00:25:15.390
Thanks.

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:16.410
That was a typo, then.

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:18.440
That's what I thought.

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:20.780
So you can do some roll
ups if you need to.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:24.500
If you need to break out
finance into individual apps,

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:28.160
or dev test prod, or whatever
other folders beneath that

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.620
for operational reasons,
you certainly can.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:32.810
And we typically
almost always roll up

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:35.060
the costs into that
top-level folder.

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:36.230
And that's what you'll find.

00:25:36.230 --> 00:25:38.460
All of my scripts that
are out there for,

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:40.220
all the queries
that are out there,

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:41.430
roll up to the top level.

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.310
But it's easy enough
to adjust, too.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:48.650
So that's a typical
corporate environment

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:52.850
that's general purpose GCP,
not real focused on deploying

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:53.870
a specific product.

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:08.230
Some sample view here, or a
screenshot, of Data Studio.

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:11.920
So we've got a number of
folders there in that dropdown.

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:13.210
So that's a filter.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:14.800
This is our standard report.

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.660
It'll look just
very similar to one

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.670
you can pull down from Google.

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:19.990
It has a date range.

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:22.330
It has a list of folders.

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:26.680
You'll see the summary dollar
amounts there on the right.

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:29.780
And then once you
dial in that cost,

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.610
you can see then all the
different graphics will change.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:36.250
So that stuff's all super easy
to do in Data Studio, as well.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:38.230
You set it up as
a filter control.

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:42.630
And you tell it what column
you want to filter on.

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:44.800
I think I forgot to
mention one thing, too.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:49.960
In BigQuery, BigQuery
supports date partitioning.

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:52.720
And that's a way to limit
how much data we're querying.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:57.550
So BigQuery is charged by
how much data you query.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:00.260
That's kind of generally
how your cost is.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:03.820
And so in here is,
date partitions

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:06.970
are filled out by the
BigQuery data export.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:11.680
And this control will use that
in Data Studio to filter it.

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:16.270
So say you had a gig of data,
or whatever number of data,

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:18.043
that covered all of time.

00:27:18.043 --> 00:27:20.210
And if you just press Search
without a filter on it,

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:21.670
it will scan all that data.

00:27:21.670 --> 00:27:23.260
And you'll pay for
that whole query.

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:27.200
But in this case, I said
February 1 to February 28.

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:30.250
And that adjusted the
date partition filter.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:33.460
And so I only paid for-- maybe
it's 10 meg of that data,

00:27:33.460 --> 00:27:34.810
or whatever the number is.

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:36.080
It's the subset of data.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.750
So I don't need
to pay for it all.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:41.290
And it does keep
things more affordable.

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:45.400
Not a huge concern for most
people in their footprint

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:47.590
of their billing data,
because it is small enough.

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:50.740
But still important to note
that that's easy enough to do.

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:52.390
And it's built in the platform.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:56.290
So why not use it and save
a little bit of money?

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:58.660
So that's the view.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.060
The standard report that
you pull down from Google

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:06.190
has broken down by
resource, by project.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:08.290
You could explore
into Compute Engine.

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:11.050
And you could see, for
example, how much you're

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:13.297
paying for a persistent disk.

00:28:13.297 --> 00:28:15.130
There's a number of
ways to break that down.

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:22.590
And so here is, then,
a similar concept

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.240
to present that in
the Sheets view.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:27.450
So the BigQuery
connector in Sheets.

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:31.110
So like I mentioned, this would
be what your finance department

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:36.710
uses to report back their
spend to the business,

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:37.980
break down those costs.

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.460
So really easy,
tabular way to do it.

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:43.290
And that's an example of
a way to group by folder.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:47.280
There's no reason you couldn't
group by label as well,

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:49.070
or both.

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:49.570
Or both.

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:51.610
And it includes sample
BigQuery of what

00:28:51.610 --> 00:28:54.820
you would put in here to
generate that as well.

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:58.530
I even did one that has last
month automatically calculated

00:28:58.530 --> 00:28:59.030
for you.

00:28:59.030 --> 00:29:00.410
So you don't need to apply that.

00:29:00.410 --> 00:29:02.830
And it would be really easy
to just stick it in a sheet

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:03.680
and start using it.

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:10.720
So a single-tenant model.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.740
These guys are the easiest to
make decisions for, I think.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:16.930
Meaning, we have
a certain amount

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:20.230
of resources that are
provisioned every time we

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:21.055
spin up a customer.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:27.780
Super obvious that we probably
need to automatically provision

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:28.540
that customer.

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:30.510
So we build some
Terraform scripts.

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:37.500
We separate out with IAM
dev test and prod, perhaps.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:40.890
And we deploy a new
project for every customer.

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:45.270
And we deploy whatever
into that project

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:46.590
that makes the customer go.

00:29:49.127 --> 00:29:51.460
Usually, the biggest decision
you're going to make there

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:52.560
is related to networking.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.600
Kind of out of scope of this
conversation, but obviously,

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:57.460
it's super important.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:58.990
Everybody with a
single-tenant model

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:01.390
always has some kind
of common ops system.

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:05.830
Monitoring, security systems,
those kind of things,

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:09.400
that it gets to be tough
to align those costs

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:13.050
with individual customers.

00:30:13.050 --> 00:30:16.340
They're just a fixed fee
that our customers have.

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:19.180
So generally speaking, we don't
associate those costs back

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:22.040
to an individual,
the ops systems.

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:24.340
But you can see there,
individual project level

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:27.610
reporting is sufficient,
generally speaking, to say,

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:31.840
how much for customer
A to run last month,

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:33.670
is a very easy
thing to search on.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:37.510
That's super obvious.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.340
We do have a lot
of people who try

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:42.100
to fit numerous customers
into one project,

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:44.190
but yet still have a
single-tenant system.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:45.970
And our default
would be to recommend

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:50.620
not to do that unless we
had a specific reason to.

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:53.290
So there's a little
bit of conversation

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:55.322
that needs to be had
when they're in that spot

00:30:55.322 --> 00:30:57.280
and try to figure out
why they want to do that.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:30:59.860
But generally, don't be
afraid to make projects.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:02.380
We have customers out there
who have hundreds of projects.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:02.980
And it's cool.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:06.220
It all fits within
the reporting scheme.

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:07.750
It doesn't cost
anything additional.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:10.750
So it's a nice, easy
divider there for you

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:12.850
to reflect those costs back.

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:23.820
The elephant here.

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:25.620
Multi-tenant systems.

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:32.280
And how do I use the tool sets
you've showed so far to reflect

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:35.100
back costs per customer?

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.410
And this is hard.

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.510
This isn't easy at all.

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:41.490
Oftentimes, it takes
a conversation about,

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:42.900
what's your architecture?

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:47.180
I see this and I assume
we have a GKE strategy.

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:49.350
But not everybody is there.

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:53.100
There's plenty of other
spots to be in for people.

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:56.520
Almost always, I'd say, let's
look at a labeling strategy.

00:31:56.520 --> 00:32:00.180
The folder idea just
isn't going to apply here.

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:01.740
It's not going to
add a ton of value.

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:03.300
We might do both.

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.300
We might do both, depending
on how we're using GCP.

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:10.650
But labels are a better
way to dial in those costs.

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:15.870
Application level monitoring
is, of course, the best strategy

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.960
for the really large fish.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:22.800
They'll be building in ways to
track how much each customer is

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:26.370
using all the different APIs,
do tracking back that way,

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:28.710
maybe look at transaction costs.

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:32.955
Probably look at a way to
monitor disk and storage usage.

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:39.520
So maybe fairly obvious,
but use the stack

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:42.110
that is friendly
for auto-scaling

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:44.697
so that the resources
can expand and contract.

00:32:44.697 --> 00:32:45.780
That applies to everybody.

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:48.780
But multi-tenant
architectures need to do that.

00:32:48.780 --> 00:32:51.510
GKE is by far the
most common choice

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.940
of how they choose to do that.

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:57.670
And then looking at monitoring
within the clusters.

00:32:57.670 --> 00:33:00.000
I'm not going to get
into, how many GKE

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.330
clusters should we have here?

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:04.080
I know that's
super hotly debated

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:06.630
as to how big we go with that.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:09.630
We know most people have
a namespace strategy

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:10.950
to try to isolate things.

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:13.590
We know we're getting more and
more comfortable with bigger

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:14.550
clusters.

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:17.460
But I wouldn't always assume,
let's go with one cluster

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:19.200
and shove everything into it.

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:22.650
That's not necessarily the plan.

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:25.800
It takes a bit of a
conversation about scale,

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:28.110
about, what are the
different services involved?

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:31.290
And almost always is a
whiteboard conversation for us.

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:35.830
So like I said, multi-tenant
cost alignment is very hard.

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.670
We've got a lot of tools
that can help you here.

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:39.900
New areas.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:47.570
Monitoring services within
GKE to try to report back

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:48.968
what's happening at that level.

00:33:48.968 --> 00:33:50.510
But in true
multi-tenant, we're still

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:51.885
going to have
customers all mixed

00:33:51.885 --> 00:33:53.812
together, transactions
all mixed together.

00:33:53.812 --> 00:33:55.520
And we need to really
build it in the app

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:59.560
to monitor what's
going on there.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:05.200
So I think that's about
all at the high level

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:08.220
that we can give you on
the multi-tenant system.

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:10.600
It would be easy enough
to build some reports off

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:13.929
of this that help you see what's
happening at the service level,

00:34:13.929 --> 00:34:16.909
for example.

00:34:16.909 --> 00:34:21.190
So common recommendations
across the board that we have.

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.389
Automate provisioning.

00:34:22.389 --> 00:34:25.090
Terraform is something
that comes up

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:28.190
every day in our world.

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:30.175
Provision things
programmatically.

00:34:30.175 --> 00:34:31.550
And then we can
make sure they're

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:32.750
put in the right folders.

00:34:32.750 --> 00:34:35.750
We can make sure they have
the right labels attached

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:39.090
and that the reports are
going to be accurate.

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:41.870
Another important recommendation
is start your folder design

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:43.100
with talking about IAM.

00:34:43.100 --> 00:34:45.005
Don't start talking
about reporting.

00:34:45.005 --> 00:34:46.880
We're approaching it
kind of backwards, then.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:49.429
We need to make sure that
we're not using folders just

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:51.469
to report on consumption.

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:56.810
And then we have a less than
ideal identity access model.

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:59.810
We need to start by looking
at, what permissions

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:04.040
do we need to dial in per
folder before we talk about,

00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:08.910
is that valuable as
a roll up for costs?

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:12.390
And a combination of labels
and folders is super common.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.130
It's not like a this or that.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:15.390
We can use both.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:23.310
So like I mentioned,
I have some example

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:25.200
queries out there on GitHub.

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:26.640
Super big, long path.

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:28.662
We don't have a million
projects on our GitHub,

00:35:28.662 --> 00:35:29.870
though, on our public GitHub.

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:35.610
So if you go to
GitHub.com/Agosto,

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:40.530
you'll see a script to go dump
out using G Cloud commands,

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:44.250
to dump out, what are my top
level folders and what are

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:45.533
their numbers?

00:35:45.533 --> 00:35:46.950
And if I feed it
the right switch,

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:49.710
I can even generate
the insert syntax

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:53.490
to dump that into a
table in BigQuery.

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:55.560
So then the other
example queries

00:35:55.560 --> 00:35:59.360
that I wrote have an
assumption that you're going

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:01.860
to have that table in place.

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:04.740
So I made the create
table statement.

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:09.940
I made several of the
required things to set it up.

00:36:09.940 --> 00:36:13.350
And then you can
generate some queries

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:16.260
and see for yourself
what the data looks like.

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:18.090
And at that point,
hopefully, it's

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:22.200
usable as a building
block for you

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:24.923
to start creating
your own reporting.

00:36:24.923 --> 00:36:27.090
Like I mentioned, I'll try
to add in this afternoon,

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:33.360
then, some links to the
template Data Studio reports.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:36.320
Maybe start with the view
that I created in there.

00:36:36.320 --> 00:36:37.522
You'll see one called View.

00:36:37.522 --> 00:36:39.480
I don't remember exactly
the title of the file.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:40.620
But it's a view.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:42.270
And if you save
it as a view, you

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:44.550
can hook up Data
Studio to that, instead

00:36:44.550 --> 00:36:46.020
of the billing data table.

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:48.510
And it will pull in your
folder names for you

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:51.840
that you can then use to filter.

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:54.030
So, hopefully, that's a
starting point for you

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:56.460
to start building some
of your own reporting

00:36:56.460 --> 00:37:00.480
and be able to present
to your different people

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:03.120
within your business
what they need.

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:05.250
So from there, the
recommendation is always,

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:07.620
talk about who needs
to see this and try

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:10.740
to identify a couple
of different groups

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:13.170
within your organization
who need reporting.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:16.200
And then start working with them
to see, what kind of exact data

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:17.400
do they need?

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:21.420
So we have those standard
Google Data Studio templates

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:22.740
that we work from.

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:25.260
And then we add
things all the time.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:26.970
The first one we add is paid OS.

00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:30.210
For anybody using paid
OSs, they need to see that.

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:32.670
So it's pretty easy
to see how that works

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:34.500
once you have the example.

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:37.260
So whether you're a
technical person like that

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:38.880
and want to dive
in, or whether you

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:40.297
want to give that
to someone else,

00:37:40.297 --> 00:37:42.000
I think we have a
starting spot for you.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:45.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

